#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050649 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429668 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 870->1475. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049879} :encompassing/weight 1.0} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429669 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 870->1482. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049053} :encompassing/weight 0.5} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429670 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 870->28. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048470} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "5c4bc59d-ac0f-5225-a803-5b20b7524274" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087689 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087494 :question/id #uuid "5e22ac55-3e84-5cb4-8231-5f882da9240e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4103" :question/problem "What is the value of $x$ if $e^{x - 3}=2^5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-3} & ={2}^{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\ln \\left( e^a \\right) = a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-3 = \\ln \\left( 2^5 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ and rewrite the right-hand side using the power law of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})+3 \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5\\ln (2) + 3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d8c6c93-9d15-5482-8130-25bb9cabf008" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087690 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087495 :question/id #uuid "ccf59ef2-a5dd-5a56-9af8-1878376dea16" :question/math-academy-id "e-4104" :question/problem "Find the solution to the equation $e^{2-4x} = 2$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(\\ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2-4x} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 2-4x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ -4x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx  \\approx 0.327\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "765ef63a-e3f0-503b-876d-3b4776b2cafe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087691 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087496 :question/id #uuid "12eedc57-11a6-540c-bdc9-bf5df777de60" :question/math-academy-id "e-4105" :question/problem "Given that $e^{x+1} + 1 = 3,$ what is the value of $x$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the exponential, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1}+1 & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x =\\ln\\left(2\\right) -1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10095048-0db6-5b4e-889f-303f07449367" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :topic/math-academy-id 870 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051065}] :topic/title "Solving Equations Containing the Exponential Function"}]}
